#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221203 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "03fbfe2c-b12f-5220-9fae-e0dfe210a505" :question/math-academy-id "e-21857" :question/problem "Find the Fourier series of a particular solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'-2y=f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim 3 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{12}{n^2\\pi}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a particular solution has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) + b_n \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y'_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-2(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -{a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{0} &=3 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n} &=\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation,\n\n$$\na_0=-3,\n$$\n\nwhich means the constant term is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation,\n$b_n=-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}.$\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}\\right)\\dfrac{n\\pi}{3}-2a_n=\\dfrac{12}{n^2\\pi}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\na_n=-\\dfrac{216}{n^2\\pi(n^2\\pi^2+36)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $b_n$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{216}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼-\\frac{3}{2} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{216}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221204 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "3944f6e4-a3a8-5bad-87c5-18227ce223bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21858" :question/problem "Find the Fourier series of a particular solution of the differential equation $y''-y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim 1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\n*Hint: We may assume that all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Because the forcing function has only a constant term and sine terms, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y''_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty -b_n n^2\\pi^2 \\sin(n\\pi x)\n-\n\\left( \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n \\sin(n\\pi x) \\right)\n=\n1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(-n^2\\pi^2-1)\\sin(n\\pi x)\n=\n1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n![](images/0e/0e33aea74f67de3091fda6d168872458efb0acdba8a4d7b472fea9da1670df27.svg)\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \n-1\n+ \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\:\n\\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^3\\pi(n^2\\pi^2+1)}\n\\cdot\n\\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221206 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/id #uuid "926606c2-1e1a-5aab-8f2e-826163a204a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21875" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $y'' + 9\\pi^2 y = f(x),$ where the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nWhat harmonic of the forcing function does the natural frequency of the system match?\nAnd what is the resonant component of a particular solution?" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+9\\pi^2 y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of\n\n$$\n\\omega=\\sqrt{9\\pi^2}=3\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $2$-periodic function $\\left(L=1\\right)$ are\n\n$$\n\\omega_n=\\dfrac{n\\pi}{1}=n\\pi.\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have $\\omega_3=3\\pi.$ Since $\\omega_3=\\omega,$ the natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function. This is the answer to the first question.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\nf_3(x)=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_{p_3}=x(A\\cos(3\\pi x)+B\\sin(3\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))+x(-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y''_{p_3}$ and $y_{p_3}$ into the equation $y''+9\\pi^2 y=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\left[-6\\pi A\\sin(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\cos(3\\pi x)-9\\pi^2 y_{p_3}\\right]+9\\pi^2 y_{p_3}=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-6\\pi A\\sin(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\cos(3\\pi x)=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n![](images/24/245bf05fb907f9b62a62cda35b50ff309770378a611f4596e023355aec9df276.svg)\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_{p_3}=-\\dfrac{x}{54\\pi}\\cos(3\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "363a3784-86cd-5624-ae01-ecfc85592e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315435} {:answer/id #uuid "e786c74c-45b3-56a6-9fc8-0de482bd1c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316399} {:answer/id #uuid "ff24cf77-0eb8-54be-85c8-fada8a265238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "363a3784-86cd-5624-ae01-ecfc85592e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315435} :answer-field/id #uuid "562b01d0-1c00-5656-95e5-e94e170b26e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316746} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b1ab9d6-d88f-54db-8898-c8776929e416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315179} {:answer/id #uuid "623cedf2-3888-5ff9-ac59-414dab43a963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (n\\pi +5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315672} {:answer/id #uuid "7f98be46-8f13-5c49-8b77-7ff5e985b64e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(n\\pi +5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b1ab9d6-d88f-54db-8898-c8776929e416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315179} :answer-field/id #uuid "aad26612-844b-5860-be9b-50e6ab9948ab" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7431cef2-4a56-5a41-81e1-4185e67efb64" :question/math-academy-id "q-342203" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}+5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))+{b}_{n}\\cdot n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)) \\\\  & \\,+5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)) \\\\  & \\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({b}_{n}n\\pi +5{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-{a}_{n}n\\pi +5{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the cosine and sine coefficients, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2} &=0 \\\\ {b}_{n}n\\pi +5{a}_{n} &=0 \\\\ -{a}_{n}n\\pi +5{b}_{n} &=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nNext, from the second equation, \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the third equation gives\n\n$$\n-(-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{5})n\\pi +5{b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{5}\\cdot \\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b9914c2-c267-5af4-b108-3360dccddb44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315985} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e67bb4-c548-5d4b-b417-1b027f6f9f80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316076} {:answer/id #uuid "d7906531-8bc2-5207-b0f1-73878255829f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b9914c2-c267-5af4-b108-3360dccddb44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315985} :answer-field/id #uuid "9835d1ba-3f1d-500f-a45d-e5d7f26272f5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316895} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bbae417-0ede-5df6-ba34-7ec70665057a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315281} {:answer/id #uuid "2138e5a7-2bb7-594a-9669-540e6cfe651d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315303} {:answer/id #uuid "3f34cf68-1608-5551-9f22-8a15afa40311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2138e5a7-2bb7-594a-9669-540e6cfe651d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315303} :answer-field/id #uuid "acff0cef-b324-50ce-ab92-c9101787e3c9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316952} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b798cf-8651-52ca-8d7a-1e223e97ecba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315164} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba398f4-b61e-5c5f-a290-559146657e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315987} {:answer/id #uuid "9d319715-3a18-5b03-a1e3-8f19ba2b0cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315999} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5c5ed6-2f1c-5cc7-963a-f5413e330111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd5c5ed6-2f1c-5cc7-963a-f5413e330111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316349} :answer-field/id #uuid "be37d6d8-1eb7-53b0-a960-fe7553382b46" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0f6d5f3-5213-5096-b128-e5b4d23a6bdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-342229" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})) \\\\  & \\,-(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}-{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}-{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=-1 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}-{a}_{n} &=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}-{b}_{n} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=2,$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation, ${b}_{n}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2})\\frac{n\\pi }{2}-{a}_{n}=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼1 & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{8}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4568793-ef1d-5c34-9122-2cab1526c5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(n^{3}π^{2}(n^{2}π^{2} - 9))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220901} {:answer/id #uuid "378207af-1e8d-58af-8a69-7028dadf45a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (π)/(n^{3}(n^{2}π^{2} + 9))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220902} {:answer/id #uuid "4b53f1ff-e75d-5004-9c3b-b77e6eabdced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n + 1}{n^{3}π^{2} + 9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220903} {:answer/id #uuid "5832e2fb-8485-5e85-878d-4519d03a347d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (π)/(n(n^{3}π - 9))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220904}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ccee9dd8-c1e6-5562-a292-76d50f6364c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220900}] :question/id #uuid "b8b34189-42de-5d3f-89e6-c42ef8ba6b1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342349" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation $y^{″} - 9y = f(x)$, where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n$f(x)∼∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{π}{n^{3}}\\cos (nπx)$.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the sine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n$- (π)/(n(n^{3}π - 9))$ {{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af7b840f-2e0c-52c0-b79f-c78277457040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{54}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397664} {:answer/id #uuid "addf1d04-69f0-5452-bba4-8900e12fa907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397665} {:answer/id #uuid "8626a439-85ec-5b4a-9bb1-b4485f902170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8626a439-85ec-5b4a-9bb1-b4485f902170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397666} :answer-field/id #uuid "865ba785-fbd6-5a07-957c-16cb2c1df477" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397663} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a53b81f-d126-598c-8b37-6e225ea3cb17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397668} {:answer/id #uuid "f5abb3ad-0dba-5b32-98f6-df1e57e1d85e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397669} {:answer/id #uuid "0102c81a-3dd5-541b-9257-ca77f5322bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0102c81a-3dd5-541b-9257-ca77f5322bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397670} :answer-field/id #uuid "91f55656-129c-5fe5-8079-32fba851d88e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397667} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22623b70-5aab-52fc-9402-941db3c703fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397672} {:answer/id #uuid "ee445dc1-2657-5591-8683-e95785717f43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397673} {:answer/id #uuid "d51d9118-2abe-5ef4-9ada-c8a08d3b5d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397674} {:answer/id #uuid "d01d3c08-0c36-57bc-8823-3c469f701c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22623b70-5aab-52fc-9402-941db3c703fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397672} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d625df2-64a2-5d5b-b5dc-aa82f78979a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15f5a0b9-1297-5188-a047-103209518fed" :question/math-academy-id "q-343855" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-3y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-3(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{a}_{n} &=0 \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{b}_{n} &=\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=\\frac{4}{3},$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nNext, from the second equation, ${b}_{n}=\\frac{9{a}_{n}}{n\\pi }.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3(\\frac{9{a}_{n}}{n\\pi })=\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{9{a}_{n}}{n\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{n\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼\\frac{2}{3} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07607d87-94c7-5615-8410-d21177e24ce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506689} {:answer/id #uuid "6957dcc3-1d7f-5d45-8049-c8f637d3dde2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506924} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff40b82-1458-59f6-ab1d-32e43630183c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507021} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb3a7af-4a78-5819-9be4-1fc493c69627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07607d87-94c7-5615-8410-d21177e24ce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506689} :answer-field/id #uuid "09882a98-7e0e-5272-9868-612a26c52a00" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af83bf7a-2326-5550-86bc-d86a83bd79bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343874" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-4y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)-4({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine coefficients, we obtain \n\n$$\n-{b}_{n}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)=\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=-\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01560cae-7dae-59d2-abe1-84a1d7d6c454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483571} {:answer/id #uuid "4acd6ba8-bd4d-5211-b24b-b92da18d82bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483701} {:answer/id #uuid "88c5fee9-6d57-52b7-8a9a-fc2fb915b829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483848} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a5f1d7-c219-529e-8a3d-b15c3dd5908b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{n(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01560cae-7dae-59d2-abe1-84a1d7d6c454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483571} :answer-field/id #uuid "1af9059e-73b7-5797-a59d-c6b481acd050" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88bf7711-bb0b-51a9-9d4e-44874b161912" :question/math-academy-id "q-343878" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}+36y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the sine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+36({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms to find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(36-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(\\frac{324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the cosine coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{n}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}{9}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bbe36d7-183b-52f7-83e4-766f66e47bce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315183} {:answer/id #uuid "4de4ed8e-1bfe-547e-be15-ae3378443b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315562} {:answer/id #uuid "7d958b5e-134b-50cf-8131-f9c56b793d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315811} {:answer/id #uuid "98d3973b-e604-55c9-a982-051d4e5c7549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d958b5e-134b-50cf-8131-f9c56b793d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315811} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b16506d-2a67-50c2-9faa-8d223beb5eb5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316761} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a66bfc5f-3122-5a00-a1eb-aba1035e251b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316055} {:answer/id #uuid "ca234db0-bfef-5bb4-bb9c-30e03379c192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316257} {:answer/id #uuid "dccf7745-d3a5-5591-b2fd-26d0e4c875cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316347} {:answer/id #uuid "f080bbb4-00b0-52bb-883d-abd8921bc331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a66bfc5f-3122-5a00-a1eb-aba1035e251b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316055} :answer-field/id #uuid "c113cb3c-937d-5360-937b-d1f8edc5d5c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cffe765b-9372-5c49-a0ae-c98bc95f8843" :question/math-academy-id "q-343880" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-3y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the sine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})-3(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}-3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=3 \\\\ {a}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}-3) &=\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=-1 \\\\ {a}_{n} &=\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2077a0e4-25ea-549f-b18b-eeaee168ff0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368339} {:answer/id #uuid "cf025f55-d758-53ad-ba9a-e6003ea863d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368340} {:answer/id #uuid "4926bd85-afe3-53d9-b42e-08dd5f26bb22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368341} {:answer/id #uuid "70f39c4d-5178-5b56-a752-489a2b9d7ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4926bd85-afe3-53d9-b42e-08dd5f26bb22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368341} :answer-field/id #uuid "099f2aa9-cfdf-59eb-bd13-a3254f3a71f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368338} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb9c3ae4-a918-591f-9eba-4ebc030650cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368344} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fe33c1-fafa-55ee-a956-b6576f020afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368345} {:answer/id #uuid "079feac5-65d5-5c90-8e5d-94615aae162a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368346} {:answer/id #uuid "78348f45-f808-5940-ba48-bfbdb844fa02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0fe33c1-fafa-55ee-a956-b6576f020afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368345} :answer-field/id #uuid "1055c2d2-985c-506c-98b6-2c511358a08c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3769a7a2-440c-52c1-8785-e3df2dc4c9a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343885" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-6y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼6+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})-6(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))=6+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-6\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}-6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})=6+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=6 \\\\ {b}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}-6) &=\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=-1 \\\\ {b}_{n} &=\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26abab5a-e0d3-5a63-b7b9-8c8b067c042b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397692} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc27d3d-2228-5745-9f75-6ad665b3203a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397693} {:answer/id #uuid "c26489de-65c7-593c-9bc1-121a32b98a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397694} {:answer/id #uuid "efa4633c-d9b6-5177-bd10-dd0e9bfa3eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efa4633c-d9b6-5177-bd10-dd0e9bfa3eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397695} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d3c5e72-c342-555b-a87c-e14e208988df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397691} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc221971-12de-52c7-8478-0068ddd7a170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397697} {:answer/id #uuid "fe05ab27-ffe9-519c-a245-1e3105109d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397698} {:answer/id #uuid "96ad6016-80ff-5081-b817-514fd1499c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397699} {:answer/id #uuid "0e910e33-e3b2-5731-a6d5-5a16e1e9ad14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{216\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96ad6016-80ff-5081-b817-514fd1499c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397699} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fa9bba7-0c1d-559d-9dd6-0cd8ed6cec73" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d694cb78-fd17-5d72-bd5e-fb91875a20a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343891" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $1$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{36{\\pi }^{2}}=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $1$-periodic function $(L=\\frac{1}{2})$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=6\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x))+x(-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12\\pi A &=0 \\\\ 12\\pi B &=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=0 \\\\ B &=\\frac{1}{108\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368351 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f942acd-fc3f-553e-9236-b60c2312cab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368353} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5c562e-9d36-5f0d-8f3c-9b02f345cfa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368354} {:answer/id #uuid "e756ccee-191e-5f8c-ad6f-5baa620bb61c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368355} {:answer/id #uuid "0e8af9a5-eef4-597d-8a2e-bb0dd59701c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e756ccee-191e-5f8c-ad6f-5baa620bb61c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368355} :answer-field/id #uuid "df91d777-147f-5107-937e-39046d30908d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368352} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0d30efb-5e3f-5069-bdd1-edd81b1cb1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368358} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe8b930-35c5-54c4-94ff-65189b49b3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368359} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a07916-0005-53d4-80ac-6d722dff7217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368360} {:answer/id #uuid "eb23d851-2d00-5cda-9fc1-a2a5b81ab7ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fe8b930-35c5-54c4-94ff-65189b49b3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368359} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e418679-4b7e-51b6-a85a-3056974bca2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d1812d8-39e4-5dd4-b731-c6eb976d76d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-343896" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x),$ where the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A &=0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\pi }{3}B &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=0 \\\\ B &=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{3x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122300c0-651a-5232-86c6-033d51da03b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368364} {:answer/id #uuid "dd80f01e-6d8e-536a-823c-7056a3b8e1a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368365} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2cdeac-9e0a-5c78-8be5-826656897a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368366} {:answer/id #uuid "94f19c63-b8e4-5cc8-bbcf-7867a07f6258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d2cdeac-9e0a-5c78-8be5-826656897a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368366} :answer-field/id #uuid "45d93abd-3293-5cd5-b7bf-40e46fb373a3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368363} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43c19f30-8cb0-5c94-bc48-e088f92bd54d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368369} {:answer/id #uuid "505b3f74-4f8e-58d3-bd80-0663f572435a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368370} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4b72a7-1c1f-532e-95af-dd53910e34d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368371} {:answer/id #uuid "3ff458e0-f735-5d03-a067-564058720262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "505b3f74-4f8e-58d3-bd80-0663f572435a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368370} :answer-field/id #uuid "5da0e00c-bb8a-5ae4-9075-989b21ab79e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5a48baf-9ceb-513a-885b-da45d3ee0d03" :question/math-academy-id "q-343911" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\pi A &=4 \\\\ \\pi B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=-\\frac{4}{\\pi } \\\\ B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=-\\frac{4x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4163fb4e-1673-56b1-9e81-b3f9b76bdcdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506831} {:answer/id #uuid "b513ed2d-2ca6-556b-9cdc-932a781ed446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507077} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc323db-b9be-5ba3-89bf-74f4864af6f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507097} {:answer/id #uuid "ef817cb8-b7c8-5338-9ccb-b25eb7ba3fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b513ed2d-2ca6-556b-9cdc-932a781ed446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507077} :answer-field/id #uuid "955e89a0-7f01-5af1-93d8-b8760f8a0f25" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507334} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29bab42e-19fd-5999-bca5-a7f709334255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506773} {:answer/id #uuid "7380ffe2-b480-549e-b9ff-55f23f096330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506943} {:answer/id #uuid "c1abe79a-867b-5b61-b180-db62e3c89468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{32\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507105} {:answer/id #uuid "db644471-744d-5974-86ca-8c2d68c380d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29bab42e-19fd-5999-bca5-a7f709334255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506773} :answer-field/id #uuid "e28d2851-cb92-5497-aeb3-e6113495aa58" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d683633f-d60f-57e1-b38c-1931a877a7fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-343914" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x),$ where the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))+x(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,[-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A &=-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4\\pi }{3}B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=\\frac{9}{16\\pi } \\\\ B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"}]}
